"OpenCV, NumPy, TensorFlow, SciPy, cupy\n\n- SSE4.2 and AVX are instruction sets used in modern x86 processors to improve CPU computations. \n- SSE4.2 is a set of instructions that provide instructions for performing scalar and vectorized operations, and it is designed to improve performance in applications that require both scalar and vectorized operations.\n- AVX (Advanced Vector Extensions) is a set of instructions that provide instructions for performing vectorized operations, and it is designed to improve performance in applications that require both scalar and vectorized operations.\n- To make TensorFlow compile using SSE4.2 and AVX, you need to specify the `--opset` option when installing TensorFlow. For example:\n  - `pip install tensorflow --opset=2022`\n  - `pip install tensorflow --opset=2021`\n- To use SSE4.2 and AVX instructions in your TensorFlow code, you need to specify the `use_sse4_2=True` and `use_avx=True` options when creating a TensorFlow session. For example:\n  - `import tensorflow as tf`\n  - `tf.config.set_visible_devices([tf.config.list_physical_devices('GPU')[0]], 'GPU')`\n  - `tf.config.set_visible_devices([tf.config.list_physical_devices('CPU')[0]], 'CPU')`\n  - `session = tf.Session(config=tf.ConfigProto(use_sse4_2=True, use_avx=True))`\n- You can also use the `tf.config.list_physical_devices()` function to get a list of available devices, and then use the `tf.config.list_physical_devices('GPU')` and `tf.config.list_physical_devices('CPU')` functions to specify the devices you want to use. For example:\n  - `devices = tf.config.list_physical_devices('GPU')`\n  - `device = tf.config.list_physical_devices('GPU')[0]`\n  - `session = tf.Session(config=tf.ConfigProto(device=device))`\n\nNote: The `use_sse4_2=True` and `use_avx=True` options are only available in TensorFlow 2.x. In TensorFlow 1.x, you need to use the `use_sse2=True` and `use_avx=True` options instead."
